boration with various artists in the late 90s exemplified the blending of grunge and mainstream rock elements."}], "places": [{"name": "Seattle, USA", "role": "Birthplace"}, {"name": "Los Angeles, USA", "role": "Major Scene"}, {"name": "Chicago, USA", "role": "Festival Location"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: post-grunge", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "post-grunge"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q379671", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Post-grunge"], "tags": ["music genre", "Music & Compositions", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "post-grunge contributed to the historical record of the Contemporary period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Post-grunge significantly shaped the rock music landscape of the late 1990s and early 2000s, influencing hundreds of artists and generating substantial sales for the music industry, with many albums achieving multi-platinum status.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-28T17:10:33Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#30381001829", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Post-grunge is a musical offshoot of grunge that has a less abrasive or intense tone than traditional grunge. Originally, the term was used derisively and indicated mid-1990s alternative rock bands such as Bush, Candlebox, Collective Soul, Live, Foo Fighters, and Silverchair, that emulated the original sound of grunge.", "newValue": "Post-grunge emerged in the mid-1990s as a distinct musical genre that evolved from the grunge movement, which originated in Seattle during the late 1980s and early 1990s. This transformation was characterized by a softer, more polished sound compared to its raw predecessor. Bands like Foo Fighters, formed in 1994 by former Nirvana drummer Dave Grohl, and Collective Soul, which gained prominence in 1993, exemplified this shift. The genre's significance lies in its ability to retain the grunge ethos while reaching a broader audience, influencing the soundscape of contemporary rock music.

The post-grunge era was marked by commercial success and radio dominance for bands that embraced this style. Notable singles such as "Shimmer" by Fuel (1998) and "Plush" by Stone Temple Pilots (1993) topped charts, reflecting the genre's widespread appeal. The period saw a proliferation of albums that blended alternative rock sensibilities with mainstream accessibility, leading to a revival of rock music's popularity in the late 1990s and early 2000s. Festivals like Lollapalooza played a crucial role in promoting these bands, allowing them to reach diverse audiences across the globe.

